        "title": "Recap: Root Causes, Baselining and the FP Triage Loop",
        "body": "Categorizing a false positive correctly is the first step toward the right fix.\n\n### Five root-cause categories\n- **A, legitimate tooling resembling attacker behavior**: admin tools use the same mechanisms attackers abuse, so the fix is a targeted filter on the tool's signature.\n- **B, overly broad selection**: a single weak field is too common to be a signal, so strengthen the selection with a distinguishing field or a more precise modifier.\n- **C, environment drift**: new software rolled out after the rule was validated, so re-baseline and update the filter.\n- **D, data quality and field-mapping issues**: a parsing error or wrong pySigma pipeline mapping, so it needs a pipeline or parsing fix, not a filter.\n- **E, missing context**: identical activity on a test VM and a domain controller is a severity and prioritization issue rather than a detection logic flaw.\n\n### Baselining\nBaselining profiles a rule's typical matches over a representative period. First-time-seen or rare-process-by-parent analysis compares a new event with a historical table of previously seen combinations, treating genuinely novel combinations as higher priority than combinations the environment has seen thousands of times before. A baseline tells you which patterns deserve a filter but does not replace the filter, and it must be revisited because of drift.\n\n### The worked example and the loop\nA rundll32.exe alert looked like comsvcs.dll MiniDump credential dumping, but the DLL path and hash showed a patch tool's own DLL. The fix was a targeted filter, tested against both samples, documented and linked to the investigation. The loop is: triage, root-cause, choose the matched fix, submit through pull request and CI, validate the original malicious sample still fires, then deploy and monitor. Track precision per rule, alerts per analyst per shift, the percentage of alerts closed as FP, and review the noisiest rules on a recurring cadence. A high FP closure rate across most rules suggests a systemic tuning problem.",
