        "body": "Once a false positive is root-caused, the exception can live in one of three places. The central design question is whether the exception is structural, organization-specific but durable, or purely temporary. Picking the wrong tool does not always open an immediate gap, but it creates long-term maintenance debt.\n\n### Tool 1: the inline filter block\nA filter inside the rule, combined with 'selection and not filter'. It is version-controlled with the rule and suits structural, universal noise that any deployment of the rule would see.\n\n### Tool 2: the standalone Sigma filter document\nA separate YAML document with a title, a logsource and a filter section holding three parts: **rules** (a list of rule ids), **selection** (same syntax as a rule selection) and **condition**. Because it references target rules by their unique id rather than by title or file path, the link survives a rename or move. pySigma merges the filter into the converted query, so the practical effect matches an inline filter while organization-specific logic stays out of a rule other organizations also run.\n\n### Tool 3: SIEM-native suppression\nSplunk correlation search throttling suppresses repeated notable events for the same field values within a time window. Microsoft Sentinel offers watchlists combined with automation rules that close or suppress matching incidents. These operate on the deployed platform, not the Sigma source, so they do not survive a rule being re-converted from source unless separately maintained. That makes them appropriate for temporary operational needs such as a maintenance window.\n\n### The governance risk\nSuppression added directly in a SIEM console without a ticket or documentation sits outside version control and peer review. It is easy to forget, which feeds the allowlist rot problem covered on the next page.",
