        "title": "Recap: Safe Scope, Lifecycle and Allowlist Rot",
        "body": "The key design principle for any exception is **least scope**: as narrow as the legitimate case requires and no narrower.\n\n### Ranking specificity\nFrom strongest to weakest: a full file hash, then path plus parent process plus user, then path alone, then process name alone. A path-only exception is weaker because an attacker who gains write access to that path could place a malicious binary there and inherit the exception. A process-name-only exception is functionally equivalent to disabling the rule for that name across the environment, since an attacker can rename a binary. Exceptions tied to a temporary condition should carry an explicit expiration, and justification, owner and review date are recorded at creation, not added later.\n\n### The five-stage lifecycle\n1. **Creation**: scoped, documented and linked to the justifying ticket.\n2. **Implementation and review**: as code through pull request and peer review.\n3. **Active monitoring**: confirm it still matches only the intended case.\n4. **Scheduled review** at expiration, ideally by someone other than the creator to avoid rubber-stamping.\n5. **Renewal or retirement**, removing it from the repository as deliberately as it was added.\n\n### Allowlist rot\nAllowlist rot is the accumulation of stale, overly broad or forgotten exceptions. An accumulated allowlist is institutional knowledge of exactly what detection will not catch, and a stale path exception for a decommissioned tool is a standing blind spot. Mitigations are periodic allowlist audits, mandatory owner and expiration fields enforced at creation (for example in CI) and automatic flagging of overdue reviews.\n\n### Governance\nRecord justification, owner, creation date, review date, ticket and ideally a distinct approver. Limit who can merge exceptions, because exceptions reduce visibility and deserve at least the scrutiny of new detections. Git history gives version-controlled exceptions an audit trail, while SIEM suppression needs a supplementary register.",
