        "body": "With many rules firing, severity tiering decides the order in which analysts work the queue, instead of arrival order or ad hoc judgment. Working in arrival order can mean a routine alert on a test server is handled before credential dumping on a domain controller simply because it fired first.\n\n### The five tiers\nCritical (active, high-impact compromise), High (strong likelihood of malicious intent and meaningful impact), Medium (suspicious and worth investigation), Low (minor deviation, supporting context) and Informational (logged for visibility or audit). The Sigma level field mirrors this scale, and Falcon and Sentinel use comparable ones. The level field is only the author's baseline, set once without knowing the specific asset or user.\n\n### Risk inputs\nClassical risk is likelihood and impact. SOC scoring extends it with rule confidence (from FP-rate tracking), asset criticality, user privilege and ATT&CK tactic severity (Impact generally outweighs Discovery). Sentinel's Fusion engine combines several lower-severity signals into one higher-confidence alert. No single formula is universal.\n\n### The severity matrix\nA two-dimensional matrix crosses detection confidence with asset criticality. A suspicious finding on a crown-jewel asset (a domain controller, a primary financial database) rises to High, while the identical confidence on a low-value asset (a disposable test VM) settles at Low. It is usually implemented as SIEM or SOAR enrichment that looks up the asset's criticality tag, and it is only as good as the asset inventory feeding it.\n\n### NIST categorization\nOnce an incident is confirmed, NIST SP 800-61 categorizes it by Functional Impact, Information Impact and Recoverability Effort, where Not Recoverable is the most severe. Revision 3 (April 2025) reframes response around the six CSF 2.0 functions: Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover. This works at a different level than per-alert tiering.",
