        "title": "Recap: Dynamic Severity, Design Mistakes and Triage Order",
        "body": "A single Sigma rule with one static level can and should produce different final tiers depending on context. Severity is dynamic per alert, not fixed per rule. The T1003.001 LSASS rule carries level: high, yet on a domain controller under a privileged administrator it can settle at Critical, while the same match with the same confidence on a disposable test VM under a standard user might settle at Medium. This adjustment is not written in the rule's level field. It is computed downstream in SIEM or SOAR enrichment logic, using real-time asset criticality and user privilege data the rule author could not have anticipated. It can also work downward, for example reducing priority for a near-match to a pending exception.\n\n### Common mistakes\n1. Static severity with no asset-context adjustment, which hides urgent alerts among noise.\n2. Too many tiers, which creates decision paralysis.\n3. Conflating detection confidence with business impact. A very high-confidence detection on a genuinely low-value asset should not automatically outrank a lower-confidence, more ambiguous detection on a crown-jewel asset.\n4. Never revisiting severity for a rule whose tracked precision is chronically poor.\n5. Building the matrix before the asset criticality data exists.\n\n### Triage order\nWork tiers in strict order, Critical before High before Medium before Low. Use the combined confidence-times-impact score as a tiebreaker within a tier. Apply tier-specific service-level expectations, for example a Critical alert acknowledged within minutes and a High alert within an hour. Confirmed incidents escalate into formal incident response, where NIST's impact categories take over. Every outcome feeds precision tracking and the severity model, closing the loop from authored rule to prioritized alert.",
