        "title": "Recap: What Sigma Is and How a Rule Is Built",
        "body": "This module taught you to write, harden and prove Sigma detections. Start with the idea. **Sigma** is an open, YAML-based, vendor-agnostic format for describing log-based detections, maintained by the SigmaHQ community. The point is portability: a team writes the logic once and a converter turns it into SPL, KQL, EQL or another query language, so nobody rewrites a detection by hand when the SIEM changes. The converter is a **backend** built on **pySigma**, and it needs a **pipeline**, a mapping profile for one specific product, to translate the rule correctly.\n\n### Anatomy of a rule\nA Sigma rule has three regions. The **metadata** (title, id, status, description, tags, falsepositives, level) says what the rule is and how far to trust it. The **logsource** block uses **category**, **product** and **service** together to tell the backend which telemetry to target. The **detection** block holds the named selections, the optional filters and the condition.\n\n### Fields worth remembering\n- **id** is a UUID, so exceptions can reference a rule unambiguously.\n- **status** records maturity (stable, test, experimental, deprecated), and an experimental rule should never get the trust of a stable one.\n- **tags** carry MITRE ATT&CK technique and tactic IDs, which makes rules machine-mappable to a coverage heatmap.\n- **level** is the author's baseline severity, from informational up to critical.\n- **falsepositives** lists legitimate activity known to trigger the rule, giving the triaging analyst a head start.\n\nThe dangerous mistake is a wrong or missing logsource: the YAML stays valid and the rule converts without errors, but it silently never matches real data.",
