        "title": "Recap: Selections, Conditions and Filters",
        "body": "Inside the detection block, logic follows a few firm rules. Within a single **selection**, different fields are always combined with **AND**: every listed field must match. When one field is given a list of values, the items are combined with **OR**: any one value satisfies that field. So a selection with ParentImage and a three-value Image list matches only if ParentImage equals its target AND Image equals one of the three listed values.\n\n### The condition field\nThe **condition** combines named blocks with and, or, not and parentheses. Two shorthands replace long chains of or. The pattern '1 of selection*' means OR across every block whose name starts with selection_, while 'all of selection*' means AND across that same group. 'all of them' requires every selection in the rule to match. When you mix and with or, group explicitly with parentheses, because an ungrouped mix is easy to misread even when the parser has a defined precedence.\n\n### Filters\nA **filter** uses the same syntax as a selection but is subtracted with the pattern 'selection and not filter'. Inline filters are for structural noise that any deployment of the rule would see. Organization-specific exceptions do not belong hard-coded into a shared rule.\n\n### How precise a filter must be\nA weak filter, for example one matching only on Image, can silently blind the entire rule if an attacker names a malicious binary the same as the legitimate tool being excluded. Strong filters combine multiple fields (path, parent process, and ideally a file hash) so the exception only ever matches the specific legitimate behavior. After adding any filter, re-test both the malicious sample and the newly excluded benign sample.",
