      {
        "pageNumber": 3,
        "title": "Recap: Field Mappings and Common Anti-Patterns",
        "body": "Sigma's field names (Image, CommandLine, ParentImage, TargetFilename) form a generic, backend-agnostic taxonomy, modeled on Sysmon's field names because Sysmon was the most common Windows telemetry source when Sigma was designed. A **pySigma pipeline** renames each generic field into the product's real field and translates the logsource into a concrete index or table. One Sigma field can even map to several real fields: in Microsoft Defender, Image splits into FolderPath and FileName, CommandLine becomes ProcessCommandLine, and ParentImage becomes InitiatingProcessFolderPath. The sigma-cli tool wraps pySigma, with -t naming the backend and -p naming the pipeline.\n\n### Anti-patterns to avoid\n- **Too broad**: matching only Image ending in powershell.exe fires on nearly every invocation. Layer several weak signals instead.\n- **Hard-coded paths**: 32-bit tools live under Program Files (x86). Using contains or endswith modifiers on the distinctive suffix of a path is usually safer than a full literal match.\n- **Wrong logsource**: silent non-detection.\n- **Empty falsepositives and default level**: tells the next analyst 'we don't know'.\n- **Ambiguous grouping** of and/or without parentheses.\n- **Testing only the malicious sample**.\n\n### From ATT&CK gap to rule\nDetection engineering usually starts from a coverage gap. For T1003.001 (LSASS Memory), the telemetry is Sysmon Event ID 10 (ProcessAccess), where a SourceImage opens a handle to a TargetImage with a GrantedAccess value such as 0x1010, 0x1410 or 0x1FFFFF. WerFault.exe and security agents also touch lsass.exe, so they become filters. Then test before shipping.",
        "keyPoints": [
          "Sigma field names are a generic taxonomy; a pipeline maps them to a product's real fields and tables",
          "One Sigma field can map to several real fields (Image becomes FolderPath and FileName in Defender)",
          "Prefer endswith or contains on a distinctive path suffix over a full literal path",
          "LSASS credential dumping (T1003.001) is detected with Sysmon Event ID 10 and GrantedAccess values, with filters for WerFault.exe and agents"
        ]
      },
      {
        "pageNumber": 4,
        "title": "Recap: String, List and Regex Modifiers",
        "body": "Modifiers are pipe-separated suffixes on a field name, written field|modifier: value. They change how the value is compared with the field, not which field is examined. They fall into families: string position, list logic, regex, encoding-aware, command-line normalization and structural comparisons. Their job is precision: avoiding both false negatives (missing the attack) and false positives (matching too broadly). Multiple modifiers can be chained on one field when a value needs more than one transformation.\n\n### String position\n- **contains** matches anywhere in the field.\n- **startswith** anchors to the beginning, useful for a suspicious directory root.\n- **endswith** anchors to the end and is the standard way to match a binary name across several install paths.\n\nWhen a list of values uses the same modifier, the default is OR: any one value matches.\n\n### The all modifier\nThe **all** modifier changes the list logic from OR to AND, so every listed value must be present simultaneously. It is typically paired with contains to require several independent suspicious command-line fragments together. Using all when OR was intended makes a rule too strict, so always check a rule for the all suffix before assuming how a list behaves.\n\n### Regex with re\nThe **re** modifier treats the value as a regular expression, with anchors, quantifiers, character classes, alternation and grouping. Regex matching is case-sensitive by default, and the i flag makes it case-insensitive (the m and s flags change anchor and newline handling). Use re only for genuinely structured patterns, and test the converted query, because regex behavior can differ across backends.",
        "keyPoints": [
          "contains matches anywhere, startswith anchors to the start, endswith anchors to the end",
          "A list of values on one field is ORed by default; the all modifier makes it AND",
          "The re modifier is case-sensitive by default; the i flag makes it case-insensitive",
          "Regex is a precision tool for structured patterns and must be tested on the converted query"
        ]
      },
      {
        "pageNumber": 5,
        "title": "Recap: Evasion-Aware and Structural Modifiers",
        "body": "Two modifiers exist because attackers try to defeat naive string matching. **base64offset** handles base64 alignment. Base64 encodes data in 3-byte groups, so the same substring produces different base64 text depending on where it falls in a larger blob. There are exactly three possible alignment shifts, and base64offset pre-computes all three and matches if any appears. **windash** expands a value into every interchangeable dash variant that Windows tools accept: the hyphen-minus, the forward slash and several Unicode dashes. A windash match on '-enc' therefore also matches '/enc'. LOLBins (Living-Off-the-Land Binaries) are legitimate system tools that attackers abuse, and windash defends against flag-character substitution tricks used with them.\n\n### Chaining and order\nThe **wide** modifier (also called utf16le) reproduces UTF-16 encoding, where each character is padded with null bytes. PowerShell encodes an -EncodedCommand payload as UTF-16LE before base64, so a payload match needs wide|base64offset. Because each modifier transforms the value before the next, order matters. The **cased** modifier forces case-sensitive matching for the rare cases where case itself is the signal.\n\n### Structural modifiers\n- **fieldref** compares a field with another field in the same event.\n- **cidr** matches an IP against a network range.\n- **exists** checks presence (true) or absence (false) of a field.\n- **gt, gte, lt, lte** do numeric comparisons.\n\n### The worked example\nEncoded PowerShell detection combines windash for the flag with wide|base64offset for a payload fragment, joined by and. It resists two independent evasions, but it only matches a known fragment and does not decode the payload.",
        "keyPoints": [
          "base64offset pre-computes all three alignment shifts; windash expands every Windows dash variant",
          "Encoded-command detections need windash for the flag and wide|base64offset for the UTF-16LE payload",
          "Chained modifiers apply in sequence, so their order matters",
          "fieldref, cidr, exists and gt/gte/lt/lte add structural comparisons beyond string matching"
        ]
      },
      {
        "pageNumber": 6,
        "title": "Recap: Testing Rules and Measuring Coverage",
        "body": "A syntactically valid rule is not a working detection. The lab separated two questions. **Does this one rule work?** is answered by testing. **How much of our attack surface do all rules cover together?** is answered by coverage mapping.\n\n### Testing one rule\nThe sigma check command validates YAML structure and specification compliance, including that the condition only references blocks that exist. It says nothing about matching real data. A proper test corpus holds **positive samples** (real or realistic malicious behavior) and **negative samples** (benign but similar-looking activity such as WerFault.exe accessing lsass.exe). A rule passes only when it matches every positive sample and none of the negative samples. A rule that misses a positive sample needs a broader or corrected selection; a rule that matches a negative sample needs either a tighter selection or an added filter. Then retest.\n\n### Detection-as-code and coverage\nRules live in version control, go through pull requests and run in a CI pipeline that can enforce level, falsepositives and ATT&CK tags. Because tags are version-controlled, the ATT&CK Navigator layer (a JSON file rendered as a heatmap) can be regenerated automatically. sigma2stix converts the same tags into STIX 2.1 objects for other tools. Tag the most specific sub-technique available.\n\n### Reading the heatmap\nA common scale is 0 (no coverage), 1 (rule exists, unvalidated), 2 (validated and deployed) and 3 (confirmed against live telemetry or a purple-team exercise). Green means a rule was tested against available samples, not that every variation is caught. Prioritize gaps by threat relevance, blast radius, compensating controls and telemetry availability. A gap cannot be closed by writing a rule if no telemetry source ever records the underlying behavior.",
        "keyPoints": [
          "sigma check validates structure only; a rule passes testing when it matches all positive and no negative samples",
          "Failing a negative sample means tightening the selection or adding a filter, then retesting",
          "Coverage scores run from 0 (no rule) to 3 (confirmed against live telemetry), and green does not mean fully solved",
          "A coverage gap with no telemetry source needs a telemetry investment before any rule can close it"
        ]
      }
