        "pageNumber": 6,
        "title": "Recap: IR Playbooks and the Full Loop",
        "body": "The **incident response plan (IRP)** is strategic and applies to every incident: who can declare an incident, roles, escalation, and legal and communications duties. A **playbook** is tactical and scenario-specific, a step-by-step procedure for one category such as phishing, ransomware, insider threat, data exfiltration, DDoS, or Business Email Compromise. A playbook always operates inside the IRP's authority structure.\n\n### Anatomy and decision trees\nA playbook has trigger and scope, roles, phase-by-phase actions, decision points, escalation criteria, and communication templates. A decision tree makes branching explicit, so a stressed responder at 2 a.m. is not parsing prose such as 'assess the scope and contain appropriately'. Decision points should be tied to concrete, checkable conditions such as recipient count and account privilege level, and kept few, because a tree with too many branches is as hard to follow as prose. Anything more nuanced belongs to the Incident Commander's escalation path.\n\n### SOAR and testing\nSOAR platforms turn mechanical steps into automated actions. Low-risk, reversible steps such as enrichment and ticket creation suit full automation, while higher-impact steps such as isolating a production host need a human-in-the-loop approval gate. A playbook is a hypothesis until tested: tabletop exercises validate roles and logic verbally, purple team exercises run real techniques against a controlled environment, Atomic Red Team provides small ATT&CK-mapped tests, and MITRE CALDERA chains techniques into longer emulations.\n\n### Keeping the loop closed\nPlaybooks are version-controlled with a named owner, reviewed periodically, and updated from postmortem action items. That pipeline is the lifecycle loop in practice: Preparation builds the playbook, Detection and Analysis and Containment execute it, and Post-Incident Activity improves it for the next responder.",
