        "title": "Recap: Detection Maturity With SOC-CMM and DeTT&CT",
        "body": "**Detection maturity** asks how the organization reaches and sustains its coverage: a repeatable, well-governed discipline, or individual heroics that would collapse if a key person left. Two SOCs can report identical coverage percentages while one relies on a documented, repeatable process and the other on one analyst who remembers which rules need attention. A maturity model tells them apart; a coverage number cannot.\n\n### SOC-CMM\n**SOC-CMM** is a free self-assessment model covering aspects grouped into five domains: business, people, process, technology and services. Each aspect gets two separate scores. **Maturity** is a six-level scale from 0 to 5 describing how formally defined and consistently executed the aspect is. **Capability** is a four-level scale from 0 to 3 describing how effectively it actually performs. Low maturity with high capability is fragile, a single point of failure; high maturity with low capability is a documented process nobody follows effectively.\n\n### DeTT&CT's three dimensions\nDeTT&CT scores **data quality** (device completeness, data field completeness, timeliness, consistency and retention), **visibility** (access to enough reliable data sources) and **detection** (the -1 to 5 scale). A low detection score with high visibility and good data quality is a rule-writing backlog item, while a low detection score with low visibility is a much larger data-onboarding project, so the dimensions must not be collapsed into one number.\n\n### Assessing and prioritizing\nBoth are self-assessments, scored conservatively with documented evidence, since inflated scoring yields a roadmap that solves problems that do not exist. Roadmap priorities are: fragile high performers first, even though they look fine today; foundational visibility gaps before detection logic refinement; and process gaps that block the purple team program's own repeatability. Most organizations reassess SOC-CMM and DeTT&CT maturity annually or semi-annually, while continuing the faster quarterly cadence for the KPI and heatmap-delta cycles. Pitfalls include maximizing maturity for its own sake, writing documents only to raise a score, assessing once and never again, and assessing in isolation from the program's existing data.",
